Z||000003||Mach10||08-11-2001||02:47 PM||||Ah, so the Fiero's a Microsoft product, then: "Nononononooo, your breaks aren't broken. That's just a device we, er, put in to, uh, ensure that while you drive, you... you... you get the chance to stretch your right leg... Yeah! That's it!"||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-11-2001||02:49 PM||||Where exactly does the brake pedal stop at under load. It should be even with the gas pedal or higher. <P>Lower would indicate either a problem with a caliper or the quick takup flow valve in the MC. <P>Excesive clearance between the pads and rotor can drop the pedal. This can be either a caliper or a wheel bearing problem. Rear calipers with hand brake issues are great for this.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||08-11-2001||03:07 PM||||Hey, now that you mention it, my handbrake DOESN'T work. I tried tensioning it once, but the adjustment nut was stuck on so tight, I stripped a small part of that threaded rod. I never touched it since...||||reg||4
Z||000006||cadaver||08-11-2001||04:49 PM||||Your all asumming that who ever worked on it before did everything right and its just worn parts.<P>The actuator rod that connects the pedal to the master cylinder may be adjusted too short too.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||08-11-2001||04:55 PM||||From what I can tell it's at 3/4 loose adjustment, but the nut is corroded into place. I don't have anything that seems to grip the threaded rod properly without starting to slip. The E-brake DID work for the safety, enough to pass, but it will not hold the car on anything other than a VERY minor slope. I have 3/4 of the rod (minus about 1/4" that needs to be cleaned up, from a slipped wrench) to play with, so I should be able to adjust it, if I ever figure out a way to free up that nut. Penetrating fluid didn't seem to help. Maybe I should coat it once a day for a week, and see if that'll do it?<P>*edit*<BR>Sorry, missed your question. I can't hit the floor with it, if that's what you mean. It's really soft at the top, but quickly builds tension. When pumping the pedal it behaves like everyother power-assist I've tried. Gets harder to push as vacuum leaks down. It's very consistant. Pumping up the brake doesn't change things, like I've seen on a dying master cyl. Stamping hard, it stays hard, and doesn't sink, like in an air-bubble. It's just loose. I'd explain it like the pads had too far to go before they hit a load. I'd adjust the rears, if it were not for that problem I described. Any pearls of wisdom on this?<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||08-12-2001||08:41 AM||||Fiero rear calipers are ajusted by the e-brake.<P>It needs to be functioning properly and well for the rest of your system to work.<P>Rebuilt calipers (both I recomend...) time :(  or maybe time for better brakes...)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000009||Smoooooth GT||08-12-2001||10:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Fiero rear calipers are ajusted by the e-brake.<P>It needs to be functioning properly and well for the rest of your system to work.<P>Rebuilt calipers (both I recomend...) time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  or maybe time for better brakes...)<P><BR>HHmmm...  When I got the 2m6 that is now the Smooth GT project had the ebrake cable cut from behind the car ..  They cut the section out that is in between the strut,spring assem.  Am I %&^%ed now??  I have a auto.. I never use it anyway's??  Steve<P>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>NEW EMAIL..  Smooth_gt@k.st<BR>ADD on's Now...  Shaun's NOS Kit.. :)<BR>Future add's.. 2" drop, 17" wheel's, finish the turbo T-3watercooled project, ViperYellow         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_3.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||08-12-2001||01:09 PM||||As I understand it, the e-brake system  only takes up slack. You'd need for the pads to fully engage to ratchet to the new setting. In my case, the e-brake cable hangs limp, and the lever will not take enough slack to engage the pads. My Chilton tells me that you have to set the base, so to speak, using the adjuster on the cables. After you get some tension, the system should maintain the new setting, and tighten as the pads wear in.<BR>Ogre: Am I far off?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>EDIT:<BR>Oh, and I'm not so sure my calipers are at fault. When the system engages, all brakes bite fine. Slam the brake quickly on dry pavement, and you get 4 skid marks, nice and even with each other. The calipers release fine, there's no dragging. The only other problem is the cheap pads that someone used in the past, cos' they squeak when you come to a stop. Oh, and one of my rotors has a deeper score mark. That's ok, 'cos I'm going to replace my rotors when my brake pads go. Pad wear is even on both sides, front and rear. <p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000011||MD_Fiero||08-12-2001||01:15 PM||||i have the same problem!!!!<P>only its in my gas pedal...not brake...<P><BR>anytips to fix it? ...TIA!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||08-12-2001||01:56 PM||||There is a GM TSB with proceedure for the cables. I'll sumarize it here. <P>1. This assumes the cables are good!<BR>2. The caliper slide bolts and stops must be free and well lubricated.<BR>3. The innner pad clips must be holding the pad back against the piston. Missing/damaged pad clips must be repalced/repaired for proper rear brake operation.<P><BR>Make sure the handle is all the way down and that no cable binds.<P>remove the rear wheels and lock the rotors in place with 2 lug nuts and some washers.<P>tighten the cables until the arms just pull off the stops on the calipers. <P>let the adjuster off until the arms just rest on the stops.<P>Once that's done see how the hand brake works and Make Certain that the pads don't drag after the adjustment. If the hand brake still won't work or the pads drag then you may have to repair or replace the caliper.<P>It is critical that the pads DO NOT drag the rotor!<P>When working correctly, the parking brake should go tight between 5-8 clicks of the handle. The car should not roll at that tension.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||HummelHund||08-12-2001||02:53 PM||||For the gas pedal, replacing the throttle cable did wonders for my Fiero. It's a huge pain to get into place (just the part that connects to the pedal) and you have to pretty much dismantle the center part of your interior, but it's worth it. The cable doesn't cost all that much, either, I got mine from the Fiero Store.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe, The "Black Wedge of Death" ($150 running car)||||reg||1
Z||000014||MD_Fiero||08-12-2001||04:12 PM||||Ok, thanks alot! =)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||08-16-2001||03:06 AM||||Thanks Ogre. I'll try that when I have a spare hour. Any ideas how to free up the frozen adjustment? Or should I just let it swim in WD-40 for a couple of hours?||||reg||6
Z||000016||sloth85GT||08-16-2001||07:07 PM||||I have that VERY same problem, my e-brake doesn't work at all, and I believe that it's just the ebrake cable that stretched. And it just needs tightening. Only the adjuster underneath the car is basically rusted into one piece. That nut will NOT move. What can I do?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">  <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT<P>WITH THE NEW WHEELS!!!!! WOOHOO!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||08-16-2001||07:21 PM||||The first thing you need to do is check the clearance between the rear pads and the rotor. They should be close but not quite touching. The inner pad will usually have a tiny bit more space than the outer.<P>If there is a big gap then the cables won't do a thing no matter how you adjust them or replace them etc...<P>by close the outter pad should be barely off the rotor, the innner should allow a couple sheets of paper.<P>There is a procedure for setting the clearance on the pads. It's in a GM TSB. It gives a way to force the caliper to adjust then it's being stuborn for whatever reason.<P>I'm working on getting it into simple terms. I have to run the list against my own calipers in the next few days. I should have it up early next week.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Mach10||08-20-2001||05:23 AM||||Well, I pulled the wheels off the back to strip and polish my wheels. While I was at it, I inspected the brake system.<P>Well, now I know why the brakes were acting funny. The top bolt on which the slide pin glides on was loose, and almost off. I could grab the caliper and rock it easily 1/2" either way with my bare hands :wft:  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><P>After sitting down, and trying NOT to think of my recent visit to the mountains, I put the damn thing back together. The Ebrake is still off, but it's grabbing better. It need adjustment. My brakes work GREAT now. Just goes to show. CHECK YOUR DAMN BRAKES EVERY TIME THE WHEEL'S OFF!||||reg||11
Z||000019||theogre||08-20-2001||07:52 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>I'm not sure if those are suposed to get locktight in the threads or not. Either way the big issue is gatting them tight to the right torque. Some guys use anti sieze on the threads... I'd never do that. A bit of the re-entrant locktight, blue I think, probly won't hurt them. ||||reg||4
Z||000020||84Bill||08-21-2001||08:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>CHECK YOUR DAMN BRAKES EVERY TIME THE WHEEL'S OFF!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>CHECK / Grease the slides too!||||reg||14
